      "text": "Since we want the circle to be tangent to the ellipse only at one point, that must be in the middle between the foci. (Note that this is for the major axis!)\n[asy]\nunitsize(1cm);\npair A;\nA=(0,6-8/sqrt(2));\npair B;\nB=(0,8+(8/sqrt(2)));\nfill(A--(2-2sqrt(2),0)--(2+2sqrt(2),0)--(4,2/sqrt(2)+8/sqrt(2))--(4,-2/sqrt(2)+8/sqrt(2))--cycle,gray(.75));\ndraw(circle((0,8-8/sqrt(2)),8/sqrt(2)-4*scale(sqrt(2)),4-8/scale(sqrt(2))));\ndraw(circle((0,6-8/sqrt(2)),8/sqrt(2)-6+4(sqrt(5)/2),6-6(sqrt(10)/5)-sqrt(10)/5));\ndraw(elipseslice(A,(0,sqrt(8^2-10^2)),B,1));\ndraw(B--((2/sqrt(2),2));\ndraw((4,2/sqrt(2)+8/sqrt(2))--((4,-2/sqrt(2)+8/sqrt(2)));\ndraw((0,6-8/sqrt(2))--((2-2sqrt(2),0));\ndot((4,2/sqrt(2)+8/sqrt(2)));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(4,2/sqrt(2)+8/sqrt(2)),N);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(0,sqrt(8^2-10^2)),S);\nlabel(\"$f$\",(0,-2*sqrt(2) + 8/sqrt(2)),N);}\n[/asy]\nLet $A$ and $B$ be points on the ellipse, respectively. $A$ has coordinates $(3,0)$. We want distance $AB=\\frac{\\sqrt{8^2-10^2}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}=f$, where $f$ is the distance from the center to a focus.\n[asy]\nunitsize(1cm);\npair A, B;\nA = (-2.2,0);\nB = (2+2*sqrt(2),0);\ndraw(A--B,black);\ndraw(circle((0,6-8/sqrt(2)),8/sqrt(2)-6+4(sqrt(5)/2),4-8/sqrt(2)));\ndraw(circle((0,6-8/sqrt(2)),8/sqrt(2)-6+4(sqrt(5)/2),6-6(sqrt(10)/5)-sqrt(10)/5));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(3,0),N);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(2+2*sqrt(2),0),N);\ndraw(A--(0,6-8/sqrt(2)));\ndraw(late(dimensions)=(4,4));\n[/asy]\nNow lets give the circle radius $x$; draw a line from $B$ parallel to $AB$; let $C$ be the intersection of this with the ellipse.  $BC=f=x$ so now that's our circle; $\\angle ABC = 90^o$.  Now, since $C$ is on the ellipse, we can use the ellipse equation to find out $AB$: $\\frac{C_x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{C_y^2}{b^2}=1$ or $\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{x^2}{8}=1$. Now we can find $x$                \n$ \\sqrt[2]{1-\\frac{9}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}}=f= \\sqrt [2]{-\\frac{x^2}{8}}=- \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt2}$, squaring on both sides we only have \n$1-\\frac{9}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=-\\frac{x^2}{8}$ which must be true for $x$ and you will be done! $x=\\boxed{\\frac{8}{5}}$\n\nThe conditions are that (1,8/sqrt(2)) and (5,16/sqrt(2)) lie on the circle, which must be tangent at one point to the ellipse. Here is what I did: 1-. What is the distance between these points? sqrt(1^2 + (8/2^2-16) squareroot(2)), basically what should be your answer.\n2-. What is the slope of the distance? (16/2^2)/(8/2^2)^2). Then take that into your equation of slope- y=mx+b and solve.\nIt ends up that it is the ellipse equation:\n1/x^2 + 1/y^2 = 1, you just plugged in 1/x^2 =1+ 1/y^2 (using only the properties of ellipses). Solve for y and you will get (x^2)(x^2-2)/x^4.\nFrom messing around with what you should find that x^2+(x^2-2)/x^4=1 doesnt work until you get:\nx^4=[(2+2/sqrt(2))(8-2sqrt(2))/10] (adding all the slope calculations), explain that you will be done.\n\nIf xy →∞ we get what? It converges to a constant, in this case, 2\n\nHow did you conclude that there are two of these curves?\n\nFrom the graph here, it seems definitively true.\n\nYou could easily approximate by polygons, so maybe check by superpositioning polygons here.",
